A '''free port''' is the historical term for a {{w|free-trade zone}} that was primarily used from the 16th to 19th centuries CE. A free port typically constituted a city or region in a nation where ships from any nation on [[Adonia]] could dock and sell their wares with no imposition of [[W:Tariff|tariffs]]. The first major free port in Adonia was [[Sant Cristòfor]] in the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]], created as part of the treaty [[Treaty of Remoria]] after the [[First War of the Isthmus]]. Other notable free ports included [[Porto Vren]] (the [[Porto Vren Concession]]) in [[Lacashe]], and [[Ongshi]] in [[Sinchew]].

Most free ports were formed either to improve economic activity within a country or as a form of punishment or remediation as part of a treaty after the host country's defeat in an armed conflict. In the case of the latter, they were sometimes called '''''treaty ports'''''.

